-- title:
--   Inertia displacement σ P-P is toric above p
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ with $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ is in the image of $A$, equipped with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $A \to L \to K$, and let $j \in K$ be a nonzero element whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular $j$-invariant. Write $X =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) for the two-chart model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ attached to these data, assumed proper.
--
--   **Geometric special fibre.** Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ which is an $A$-algebra, and let $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral. Let $i_1, i_2$ be closed immersions of $C_1, C_2$ into the base change $X_k = X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec} k$ over $k$. The hypothesis `hcover` states that every point of $X_k$ lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` states that the scheme-theoretic intersection $C_1 \times_{X_k} C_2$ is reduced; and $n$ is a positive natural number with $\operatorname{Nat.card}$ of that intersection equal to $n$.
--
--   **Sections.** $\varepsilon$ is a section of $X$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ are sections of $c_1, c_2$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$, with `hε₁` requiring that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ be the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   **Relative Picard data.** $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for $X$ over $A$, i.e. a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section; `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents, with $\varepsilon$-rigidification, the subfunctor `algEquivZeroCut` of fibrewise algebraically trivial rigidified line bundles on $X$; `hsm` and `hsep` require $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to be smooth and separated. The hypothesis `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement for $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k$ over $X_k$ with the base-changed section, and `hPk` asserts that its Poincaré bundle is isomorphic to the $k$-base change (via `BaseChange.ofR`) of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` along the first projection of $D.\mathrm{toBase} \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec} k$. Likewise $D_1$ and $D_2$ represent the analogous subfunctors for $c_1, \varepsilon_1$ and $c_2, \varepsilon_2$ (hypotheses `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`).
--
--   **The second projection.** $\nu_2$ is a morphism from $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ to $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $k$, and `hν₂` requires, for every $k$-scheme $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, an isomorphism between the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂.some` along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ and the rigidification along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` of the pullback along `curveChange i₂` of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$.
--
--   **Generic fibre over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.** $A$ and $L$ are algebras over $\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\,\mathbb{Q}$-compatibly in a tower. $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (a proper smooth integral curve with a bijection `placeOfPoint` between its closed points and the places of that field), $e_\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M_\eta.C$ onto $X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The hypothesis `Mη_chart_nonempty` requires the preimage under $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection of the image of the finite chart to be nonempty, and `hMηpin` pins this identification on $q$-expansions: for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135), the element of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` obtained from the germ of $a$ at the generic point transported by $M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$ has Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the expansion of $a$ in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance of the place dictionary: for every $g \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ acting trivially on the image of $L$, and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.C$ such that the image of $x'$ in $X \times_A \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ under the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by that of $x$, one has $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x' = \mathrm{arithmeticGalois}(g) \cdot M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x$.
--
--   **Combinatorial model of the special fibre.** $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9), consisting of abelian groups $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus}$ of $G.J_{0s}$, and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj} : G.J_{0s} \to G.J_I \times G.J_E$ with kernel $G.\mathrm{torus}$. The bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$ with the $k$-points of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, of $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$ and of $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ respectively; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` require each of them to be additive in the sense that the Poincaré bundle pulled back along a sum is isomorphic to the tensor product of the pullbacks; and `hproj` requires, for every $x \in G.J_{0s}$, that `ptsI` of the first component of $G.\mathrm{proj}\,x$ be `pts x` followed by `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some`, and `ptsE` of the second component be `pts x` followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   **Identification of the two components.** $w$ is an [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16) (a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ together with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction to $k$ is nonzero), and $\mathrm{Mdl}_1, \mathrm{Mdl}_2$ are curve models over $k$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), together with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ and $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₁`, `he₂`).
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi dictionaries.** `gpts` is a bijection from $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}) = \operatorname{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \mathrm{x1FunctionFieldBar}\,(M * p))$ onto the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$, and `hgadd` requires it to be additive for the relative group law coming from `hrep.some` and `algEquivZeroGroupCut`. Further, `hDL` is the representability statement for $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L$ over $X_L$; $\mathrm{ajL}$ is a morphism from $X_L$ to $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L).\mathrm{toBase}$ over $L$ with `hajLε` stating that the base-changed section $\varepsilon_L$ followed by $\mathrm{ajL}$ is the zero section, and `hajL` stating the Abel–Jacobi property: for every field $K'$, every $t : \operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every $t$-point $x$ of $X_L$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajL}$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the point $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of $t$ followed by $\varepsilon_L$, i.e. to $\mathcal{O}((x) - (\varepsilon))$. The morphism $\mathrm{kL}$ goes from $X \times_A \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to $X \times_A L$ and is required to be compatible with both projections (`hkL₁`, `hkL₂`), `hPL` compares the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` with the $L$-base change of the one coming from `hrep.some`, $\mathrm{ajbar} : M_\eta.C \to D.P$ is required to be $e_\eta$ followed by $\mathrm{kL}$, $\mathrm{ajL}$ and the first projection (`hajbar`) and to lie over the structure morphisms (`hajbar_over`), $\bar\varepsilon$ is a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $M_\eta.C$ mapping to $\varepsilon$ (`hεbar`) and killed by $\mathrm{ajbar}$ (`hεbar_aj`), and `hpts_aj` states the compatibility of the two dictionaries: for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $s$ mapping to $\varepsilon$ there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ equal to $(\,\text{place of } x\,) - (\,\text{place of } s\,)$ with $\mathrm{gpts}([D_v])$ equal to $x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajbar}$.
--
--   **The place and the inertia element.** $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $Pl$; $\rho : A \to Pl$ is a ring homomorphism with $Pl \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ precomposed with $\rho$ equal to $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; $\pi_k : Pl \to k$ is a surjective ring homomorphism with $\pi_k \circ \rho$ the structure map $A \to k$. Finally $\sigma$ is an automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lying in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and acting trivially on the image of $L$; $P$ and $P'$ are $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $M_\eta.C$ such that the image of $P'$ in $X \times_A \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ under the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\sigma)$ followed by that of $P$ (`hP'`); and $D_v$ is a degree-zero divisor with $D_v = \mathrm{single}(\text{place of } P') - \mathrm{single}(\text{place of } P)$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist a morphism $z : \operatorname{Spec} Pl \to D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho)$ and an element $y \in G.J_{0s}$ such that:
--
--   (i) the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\mathrm{gpts}([D_v])$ attached to the class of $D_v$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(Pl \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $z$;
--
--   (ii) the $k$-point $\mathrm{pts}(y)$, followed by the first projection of $D.\mathrm{toBase} \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec} k$, equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k)$ followed by $z$;
--
--   (iii) $G.\mathrm{proj}\,y = 0$, i.e. $y$ lies in $G.\mathrm{torus}$ since the kernel of $G.\mathrm{proj}$ is that subgroup.
--
--   This is the pointwise form, for a single geometric point, of the assertion that inertia displacements on $J_1(Mp)$ at a place above $p$ are toric: the class of $\sigma P - P$ extends to a $Pl$-section of the relative $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ scheme and its reduction lies in the kernel of the projection to the Picard groups of the two components of the geometric special fibre, that is, in the toric part. It is the step used by the statement about $\sigma \cdot x - x$ for arbitrary classes, obtained from it by summation over the points of a representing divisor.
